// credential-egress: the sequence this threat model most wants to catch.
//
// The corpus already held both halves, in different recordings and in the wrong
// shape: git-operation carries a read of ~/.ssh/id_rsa that *failed*, and
// npm-install carries connections with no credential read behind them. Neither
// exercises the relationship, and a detector for a relationship cannot be
// tested on two recordings that each contain one end of it.
//
// This recording is the relationship, with every near-miss the detector has to
// reject placed beside the real one so a rule that stopped being applied would
// change an assertion rather than pass quietly:
//
//   - ex-003 reads ~/.aws/credentials and SUCCEEDS. Rated critical. This is
//     the first half, and the only event in the stream that qualifies.
//   - ex-004 reads /etc/passwd and succeeds. Rated medium — identity, and read
//     by ordinary tooling through getpwnam — so it must NOT qualify. If it did,
//     the detector would fire on most sessions that touch the network.
//   - ex-005 reads ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 and FAILS with ENOENT. Rated critical, and
//     it must NOT qualify: an ENOENT disclosed nothing, so there is nothing that
//     could subsequently leave. Note that `sensitive_path` still charges the
//     grade on this event — the resource was touched — while the sequence
//     detector does not accept it. The two factors ask different questions and
//     this event is where that is visible.
//   - ex-006 is a DNS lookup. Egress is net.connect and net.send, the set the
//     shipped rule set names; a name resolution is not a channel out, and this
//     event must not start a search.
//   - ex-007 and ex-008 are egress the envelope GRANTED. The sequence is found
//     and reported on both, and contributes zero points, because an event a
//     grant covered scores exactly zero. The finding carries `not_charged`.
//   - ex-009 is a connection to an address no DNS answer covers, against an
//     envelope that grants net.connect by hostname. The validator cannot tell
//     whether the address is the granted host, so the verdict is indeterminate
//     rather than a mismatch. This is the event the detector changes: without
//     it the score sits below the shipped rule set's own 50 boundary and the
//     event warns; with it the score crosses and the event asks a human.
//
// Hand-authored, like the other three, and structurally faithful in the same
// way — see the corpus README. The addresses are documentation-range
// (RFC 5737) and the paths are constructed.
//
// Session s-exfil. Workspace /home/dev/project.
